<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.1. The heat equation</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

    <section xml:id="subsubsec-heat-wave">
    <title>Section 2: The Heat and Wave Equations</title>
    <subsection>
      <title>Part 2.1: Introduction to the Heat Equation in One Dimension
      </title>
      
      <introduction>
        <p>
          We'll spend some time focusing on partial differential equations and how to solve them, 
        specifically focusing on the heat and the wave equation.  In this worksheet we'll begin by focusing on the heat equation, and investigate 
        solutions and steady-states.
        </p>
      </introduction>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-heat-eqn-1d">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>heat equation</term> with constant <m>\alpha</m> and heat source <m>S</m> is:
        <md>
          \ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \alpha \nabla^2 u + S
        </md>
        where <m>\ds \nabla^2 u= \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}+\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial y^2}+\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial z^2}</m> is the Laplacian of <m>u</m>.  If <m>S=0</m> the equation is called <term>homogeneous</term>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In one dimension the heat equation simplifies to
        <md>
          \ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \alpha \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} + S.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the homogeneous partial differential equation with
    boundary conditions:
    <md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \amp  = \amp  7\frac{\partial^2
        u}{\partial^2 x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>u(0, t) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
<mrow>u(10, t) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
</md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Verify that <m>u_1(x,t) = e^{-7\pi^2t/100}\sin\left(\frac{\pi
        x}{10}\right)</m> is a solution (this means a solution to the
    differential equation and the boundary conditions).</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>Also verify that <m>u_2(x,t) = e^{-28\pi^2t/100}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi
        x}{10}\right)</m> is a solution.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Do you see a pattern that can help you identify other solutions?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now verify that <m>c_1u_1(x,t) + c_2u_2(x,t)</m> is a solution for any
        constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.  This is called the <em>principle
          of superposition</em>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the heat equation
    <md>
    \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = 4\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}
    </md>
    where <m>u(x,t)</m> is the temperature in a beam of length <m>2</m> with
    boundary conditions:
    <md>
<mrow>u(0,t) \amp  = \amp  50</mrow>
<mrow>u(2,t) \amp  = \amp  80</mrow>
</md></p>
      <p>A time-independent solution is called a <em>steady-state</em>
    solution <m>u_{ss}</m>;  that is, <m>\partial u_{ss}/\partial t = 0</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the steady-state solution <m>u_{ss}</m> to the heat equation with
    these boundary conditions.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>u</m> is a solution to the
    equation and boundary conditions above and <m>u_{ss}</m> is the
    steady-state solution that you found.  Define a new function <m>v</m>
    by <m>v = u- u_{ss}</m>.  What boundary conditions are satisfied by
    <m>v</m>?  That is, what is <m>v(0,t)</m> and <m>v(2,t)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find a partial differential equation (PDE) satisfied by <m>v</m>.  You could
    do this by expressing <m>\frac{\partial v}{\partial t}</m> in terms of
    <m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}</m> and <m>\frac{\partial^2
      v}{\partial^2 x}</m> in terms of <m>\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial^2
      x}</m> and using the fact that <m>u</m> satisfies a PDE.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that we have a beam of length <m>1</m> and
    consider the heat equation with a source
    <md>
    \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}+x.
    </md>
    and boundary conditions:
    <md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(0, t) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
<mrow>u(1, t) \amp  = \amp  100.</mrow>
</md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the steady-state solution <m>u_{ss}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use the steady-state solution to form a new equation and
    boundary conditions for the function <m>v = u-u_{ss}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
